WHAT SHIPPED, AND WHAT WAS ACTUALLY ON THE SCREEN

Every guard passed and the page served 200. Nothing in this repository can look
at a picture, so the geometry went out unverified — and rasterising the SVG and
reading it back showed four errors that no test would ever have caught.

  · Lake Ontario was drawn far too large and swallowed the right half of the
    frame, with its west end lying across the QEW and Niagara corridors.
  · Rochester floated in open water. It sits on the SOUTH SHORE of the lake,
    which is the whole reason it is reachable from the Niagara crossing.
  · Lake Erie was detached from Buffalo, which is at its eastern end.
  · The border ran off to the right doing nothing, with Rochester on the
    Canadian side of it.

Any one of those on a coverage page is worse than no map: a homeowner in
Rochester looking for their own town finds it in the lake.

THE BORDER NOW RUNS WHERE THE BORDER RUNS

Through the middle of Lake Erie, up the Niagara River between Fort Erie and
Buffalo, and out east through the middle of Lake Ontario. Drawn that way it does
the work no label can — it shows at a glance that the Niagara belt and Buffalo
are opposite banks of one river, and that Rochester is reached along the far
shore of the same lake. Drawn beside the map it was decoration.

THE FRAME IS A CROP, NOT THE COORDINATE SPACE

Spines are written in a 200×114 space because round numbers are easier to reason
about when placing a drive; the territory occupies part of it, and rendering the
whole left a third of the picture empty. The viewBox is now the content's bounds
plus the widest territory stroke.

The bounds test caught Barrie at 16.65 against a floor of 17 — the northernmost
market's halo clipping the top edge by a third of a unit, invisible in a
screenshot and obvious to arithmetic. The crop moved; the test did not.

The three GTA routes were also separated vertically. Core GTA, the 407 and the
401 east are three different drives through the same city and were rendering as
one smear.

66 guards green, 323 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline.

